   <div class="col-lg-8">
         <img class="blog-hero-image" src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="TNPSC Group I Exam Date 2026 Announced – Eligibility &amp; Pattern">
         <div class="blog-content">
            <div class="blog-body">
               <h1 class="blog-title mb-4">TNPSC Group I Exam Date 2026 Announced – Eligibility &amp; Pattern: Your Gateway to Elite Civil Services!</h1>

               <p>The Tamil Nadu Public Service Commission (TNPSC) has officially unveiled the highly anticipated schedule for the Combined Civil Services Examination – I (Group I Services) for 2026. This announcement brings clarity and a definitive roadmap for countless aspirants dreaming of securing prestigious positions within the Tamil Nadu state administration. If you're eyeing a career as a Deputy Collector, Deputy Superintendent of Police, or other esteemed Group I roles, it’s time to buckle up and strategize your preparation!</p>

               <div class="highlight-text">
                  <p><strong>Key Dates at a Glance:</strong></p>
                  <p>✅ **Official Notification Release:** June 23, 2026</p>
                  <p>✅ **Preliminary Exam Date:** September 6, 2026</p>
               </div>

               <p>This comprehensive guide will walk you through the newly announced exam dates, detailed eligibility criteria, and the revised exam pattern, ensuring you have all the essential information to kickstart your preparation effectively.</p>

               <h2 class="section-title">TNPSC Group I 2026: Important Dates to Remember</h2>
               <p>Mark your calendars! The TNPSC Annual Planner 2026 has provided the crucial dates for the Group I examination cycle:</p>
               <table class="table table-bordered">
                  <thead>
                     <tr>
                        <th>Event</th>
                        <th>Date</th>
                     </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                     <tr>
                        <td>Release of Official Notification PDF</td>
                        <td>June 23, 2026</td>
                     </tr>
                     <tr>
                        <td>Preliminary Examination Date</td>
                        <td>September 6, 2026</td>
                     </tr>
                     <tr>
                        <td>Application Start Date</td>
                        <td>To be announced with notification</td>
                     </tr>
                     <tr>
                        <td>Last Date to Apply Online</td>
                        <td>To be announced with notification</td>
                     </tr>
                  </tbody>
               </table>
               <p class="mt-3">The detailed notification, when released, will contain information regarding vacancies, application procedures, and further specific instructions. Keep an eye on the official TNPSC website, <a href="https://www.tnpsc.gov.in" target="_blank">tnpsc.gov.in</a>, for the most up-to-date information.</p>

               <h2 class="section-title">Eligibility Criteria for TNPSC Group I 2026</h2>
               <p>Before you delve into preparation, it's paramount to ensure you meet the eligibility requirements set by the TNPSC. The criteria broadly cover age limit, educational qualification, and language proficiency.</p>

               <div class="benefit-item">
                 <div class="benefit-title">1. Age Limit (as of July 1, 2026)</div>
                 <div class="benefit-content">
                    <p>Candidates must be a minimum of <strong>21 years old</strong>.</p>
                    <p>The maximum age limit for unreserved categories is generally <strong>34 years</strong>. However, age relaxations are provided for candidates belonging to SCs, SC(A)s, STs, MBCs/DCs, BC(OBCM)s, and BCMs.</p>
                 </div>
               </div>

               <div class="benefit-item">
                 <div class="benefit-title">2. Educational Qualification</div>
                 <div class="benefit-content">
                    <p>Aspirants must hold a <strong>Bachelor's degree</strong> in any stream from a university recognized by an Act of the Central or State Legislature in India. The educational pattern must be 10+2+3. Importantly, candidates in their final year of graduation are also eligible to apply.</p>
                    <p>Some specific posts might have additional preferential qualifications, such as a B.L. degree for Assistant Commissioner (Commercial Taxes).</p>
                 </div>
               </div>

               <div class="benefit-item">
                 <div class="benefit-title">3. Language Proficiency</div>
                 <div class="benefit-content">
                    <p>Proficiency in the Tamil Language (speaking, reading, and writing) is mandatory for all applicants.</p>
                 </div>
               </div>

               <div class="benefit-item">
                 <div class="benefit-title">4. Physical Standards (for specific posts)</div>
                 <div class="benefit-content">
                    <p>For certain uniformed service posts, such as Deputy Superintendent of Police, prescribed physical standards relating to height and chest measurements are mandatory. Detailed requirements will be available in the official notification.</p>
                 </div>
               </div>

               <h2 class="section-title">TNPSC Group I Exam Pattern 2026</h2>
               <p>The TNPSC Group I examination is a three-stage selection process designed to evaluate candidates comprehensively. These stages include the Preliminary Exam, Main Written Examination, and an Oral Test (Interview).</p>

               <h3>Preliminary Examination (Qualifying Stage)</h3>
               <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                  <p>✅ <strong>Mode:</strong> Objective type (OMR-based)</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Total Questions:</strong> 200 Multiple Choice Questions</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Total Marks:</strong> 300 marks (1.5 marks for each correct answer)</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Duration:</strong> 3 hours</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Negative Marking:</strong> No negative marking</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Purpose:</strong> Qualifying in nature; marks are NOT included in the final merit list.</p>
               </div>
               <p>The Prelims paper comprises 175 questions from General Studies and 25 questions from Mental Ability and Aptitude. The General Studies syllabus is aligned with a degree course, while the Aptitude &amp; Mental Ability Test questions are of SSLC Standard.</p>

               <h3>Main Written Examination (Merit-Deciding Stage)</h3>
               <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                  <p>✅ <strong>Mode:</strong> Descriptive type</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Papers:</strong> 3 General Studies papers (250 marks each) + 1 Tamil Eligibility Paper (Qualifying)</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Total Marks:</strong> 750 marks (for merit) + 100 marks (Qualifying Tamil paper)</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Duration:</strong> 3 hours for each paper</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Qualifying Marks:</strong> Minimum 40% in the Tamil Eligibility Paper is essential for other papers to be evaluated.</p>
               </div>
               <p>The Mains exam is crucial as its scores, along with the interview marks, determine the final ranking. Candidates can choose to write the mains exam in either Tamil or English.</p>

               <h3>Interview/Oral Test</h3>
               <div class="subsection-paragraph benefit-card">
                  <p>✅ <strong>Marks:</strong> 100 marks</p>
                  <p>✅ <strong>Purpose:</strong> Assesses overall personality, communication skills, decision-making ability, and suitability for administrative roles.</p>
               </div>

               <h2 class="section-title">Preparation Strategy &amp; Next Steps</h2>
               <p>With the exam date set for September 6, 2026, aspirants have a clear timeframe to structure their studies. Begin by thoroughly understanding the syllabus for both Prelims and Mains. Utilize previous year's papers and mock tests to gauge your preparation level and identify areas for improvement. Staying updated with current affairs, especially those relevant to Tamil Nadu, will be highly beneficial.</p>

               <p>The TNPSC Group I exam is a challenging yet rewarding endeavor. With the official dates and patterns now announced, dedicated preparation, consistent effort, and a strategic approach will be your best companions on this journey. Good luck!</p>
            </div>
         </div>
   </div>

            
            
